* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    x = !create_user(username)
*
*    create linux user
*
*    This function sets the STATUS() value to 0 if successful and returns true
*    Otherwise sets status to the os status and returns false
*
* 0 success
* 1 can't update password file
* 2 invalid command syntax
* 3 invalid argument to option
* 4 UID already in use (and no -o)
* 6 specified group doesn't exist
* 9 username already in use
* 10 can't update group file
* 12 can't create home directory
* 14 can't update SELinux user mapping
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function create_user(username)
$catalog !create_user
$include int$keys.h

deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'

user_created = @false

* 09 Sep 26 dm - PRE_RELEASE 22.  ***NO GATE, AND GLOBALLY CATALOGUED.***  Same
*   shape as SET_PASSWD - read the reasoning there, it is not repeated.  The
*   line below is OS.EXECUTE "sudo useradd -m " : username, and until now the
*   only thing standing in front of it was CREATEA's administrator check on the
*   VERB.  A "!" catalogued function is reachable from ordinary BASIC, measured.
*
*   STATUS 5, NOT 6, AND THE DIFFERENCE FROM SET_PASSWD IS DELIBERATE.  The
*   header above is useradd's exit codes and 6 already means "specified group
*   doesn't exist"; 5 is unused by useradd AND is the port's own "access
*   denied" (its CREATE_USER:38).  SET_PASSWD documents PAM codes instead,
*   where 6 is PAM_PERM_DENIED - so each file uses the code that is correct in
*   ITS OWN table.  A number that is wrong in the table a reader will look it
*   up in costs more than an asymmetry that is written down.
if not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)) then
   set.status 5                   ;* access denied - not an administrator
   return user_created
end
set.status 0
* Modified by Composer AI - 2026/06/10.
* Use shared !valid_os_name before OS.EXECUTE (phase 2).
* os.name.chars = '...'
* if username = '' or convert(os.name.chars, '', username) # '' or len(username) > 32 then
if not(valid_os_name(username)) then
   set.status 2
   return user_created
end
* 09 Sep 26 dm - PRE_RELEASE 14.  Through the helper; the reasoning is in
*   SET_PASSWD and is not repeated.  sd-elevate supplies -m itself and refuses
*   a name that already exists, so "sudo useradd -o -u 0 <name>" - a second
*   root - has no spelling here.
OS.EXECUTE "sudo /usr/local/sbin/sd-elevate useradd " : username
* --------------------
stat = OS.ERROR()
if stat = 0 then
  user_created = @TRUE 
end else
  set.status stat    
end 

return user_created
end
